    words_note='Before the ideas, five groups of words, so that nothing later sounds like jargon. Parameters are all the weights and biases of a model, the numbers that training adjusts, and precision is how many bits each one is stored in. Open weights means those parameters can be downloaded, under a licence that may still limit use. Open source in the OSI sense asks for more. Quantisation lowers the bits per parameter so the file shrinks. A file format such as safetensors, GGUF or ONNX packs the parameters. Inference is using the trained model, and hosting is running it on a server you rent. Keep file format and quantisation apart in your head, because mixing them up is the commonest mistake.',
    word_icons=['bars', 'key', 'calculator', 'folder', 'cloud'],
    cover_note='Welcome to Session 7. Last time you met the families of foundation models, and several of them you can download. That raises our question for today: what does it mean to own, host or rent a model? A model you can download is a very large file of numbers, and its size, its licence and its file format decide the answer. I will show real file sizes that I measured on this machine, and you will leave with a decision sheet.',
